Letting $x=-11$, we get the series:

$\begin{align}
\displaystyle\sum\limits_{n=1}^{\infty } \dfrac{(n+2)(-11+5)^n}{n\cdot6^n}
&=\displaystyle\sum\limits_{n=1}^{\infty } \dfrac{(n+2)(-6)^n}{n\cdot6^n} \\\\
&=\displaystyle\sum\limits_{n=1}^{\infty } \dfrac{(-1)^n\cdot(n+2)\cdot 6^n}{n\cdot6^n} \\\\
&=\displaystyle\sum\limits_{n=1}^{\infty }  \dfrac{(-1)^n\cdot(n+2)}{n} 
\end{align}$

By the $n^{\text{th}}$-term test, we know this series diverges.

Letting $x=1$, we get the series:

$\begin{align}
\displaystyle\sum\limits_{n=1}^{\infty } \dfrac{(n+2)(1+5)^n}{n\cdot6^n}
&=\displaystyle\sum\limits_{n=1}^{\infty } \dfrac{(n+2)\cdot6^n}{n\cdot6^n} \\\\
&=\displaystyle\sum\limits_{n=1}^{\infty }\dfrac{(n+2)}{n} \end{align}$

By the $n^{\text{th}}$-term test, we know this series diverges.
